# 📚 Array (Part 1) - Fundamentals

## 📌 What is an Array?

An **Array** is a linear data structure that stores a collection of elements of the **same data type** in **contiguous memory locations**.

Each element is identified by its **index**, allowing direct access in constant time.

---

## 🧠 Definition

> An array is a fixed-size collection of homogeneous elements stored in contiguous memory locations.

---

## 📍 Example

```text
Index :   0    1    2    3    4

Value :  10   20   30   40   50
```

Accessing elements:

```cpp
arr[0] = 10
arr[2] = 30
arr[4] = 50
```

---

# 📦 Real-Life Example

Imagine a row of lockers in a school.

```text
Locker Number

0    1    2    3    4

📦  📦  📦  📦  📦
```

Each locker stores exactly one item.

You can directly open Locker **3** without checking the previous lockers.

This is exactly how arrays work.

---

# 💾 Memory Representation

Arrays occupy **contiguous (continuous)** memory locations.

```text
Address        Value

1000  ------>   10
1004  ------>   20
1008  ------>   30
1012  ------>   40
1016  ------>   50
```

Because every element is stored next to the previous one, the computer can calculate the address of any element instantly.

---

# 📐 Address Calculation Formula

For an array:

```text
Address = Base Address + (Index × Size of Data Type)
```

Example:

```text
Base Address = 1000

Size of int = 4 bytes

Find address of arr[3]

1000 + (3 × 4)

= 1012
```

---

# 🔑 Characteristics of Arrays

- Linear data structure
- Stores homogeneous elements
- Fixed size after creation
- Stored in contiguous memory
- Supports random access
- Index starts from **0**
- Efficient traversal

---

# 🌟 Advantages

### ✅ Fast Random Access

```cpp
arr[5]
```

Time Complexity:

```text
O(1)
```

---

### ✅ Cache Friendly

Since memory is continuous, CPUs load array elements efficiently.

This makes arrays faster than many other data structures for sequential access.

---

### ✅ Easy Traversal

```cpp
for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
```

Simple and efficient.

---

### ✅ Less Memory Overhead

Unlike linked lists, arrays do not require extra memory for pointers.

---

# ❌ Disadvantages

### Fixed Size

Cannot automatically grow after creation.

---

### Expensive Insertion

Adding an element in the middle requires shifting elements.

```text
10 20 40 50

↓

10 20 30 40 50
```

---

### Expensive Deletion

Deleting an element also requires shifting.

---

### Memory Wastage

If the allocated size is larger than the required size, some memory remains unused.

---

# 🎯 Applications of Arrays

Arrays are used in many real-world applications:

- Student Records
- Employee Database
- Image Processing (Pixels)
- Matrix Representation
- Browser History (internally with other structures)
- Dynamic Programming
- Searching Algorithms
- Sorting Algorithms

---

# 📊 Complexity Overview

| Operation | Complexity |
|-----------|------------|
| Access | O(1) |
| Search | O(n) |
| Traverse | O(n) |

---

# 💡 Interview Tips

✔ Arrays are stored in contiguous memory.

✔ Array indexing starts from **0**.

✔ Arrays provide constant-time random access.

✔ Arrays have fixed size.

✔ Arrays are the foundation for many advanced data structures.

---

# 📝 Summary

- Array is a linear data structure.
- Stores same-type elements.
- Memory is contiguous.
- Index-based access is very fast.
- Random access takes **O(1)**.
- Searching takes **O(n)**.
- Arrays cannot grow dynamically.
- Arrays are widely used in algorithms and interview questions.

